re a few witnesses at the scene of the crime, but no one is sure who exactly the spy was. Three possible suspects were apprehended based on surveillance video. Each suspect had a sample of DNA taken from them. The computer's keyboard was also swabbed for DNA evidence and, luckily, one small DNA sample was successfully retrieved from the computer's keyboard.  Given the three suspects' DNA and the sample DNA retreived from the keyboard, it's up to you to figure out who the spy is!  The project should have methods for each of the following:  Given a file, read in the DNA for each suspect and save it as a string Take a DNA string and split it into a list of codons Iterate through a suspect's codon list to see how many of their codons match the sample codons. Pick the right suspect to continue the investigation on. My thoughts - 3 codons is not nearly enough to match DNA samples... but, regardless, here is the program:","topics":[],"score":214921,"global_rank":252388,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":38347},{"name":"kmussar/Bank_Account_CodeAcademy","owner":"kmussar","html_url":"https://github.com/kmussar/Bank_Account_CodeAcademy","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"This is a simple excercise demonstrating classes and objects. It is modeled after a bank account. The methods included are: accepting deposits and withdrawls, showing the balance, and showing the name of the account. Included are checks to make sure that funds are available in the account before they can be removed.","topics":[],"score":214921,"global_rank":252389,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":38348},{"name":"kmussar/RGB_Hex_Converter_CodeAcademy","owner":"kmussar","html_url":"https://github.com/kmussar/RGB_Hex_Converter_CodeAcademy","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"This project is an exercise to practice using bitwise operators. It is a calculator that can convert RGB values to Hexadecimal (hex) values, and vice-versa.  The program (1) prompts the user for the type of conversion they want, (2) asks the user to input the RGB or Hex value, (3) uses Bitwise operators and shifting in order to convert the value, and (4) prints the converted value to the user. ","topics":[],"score":214921,"global_rank":252390,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":38349},{"name":"kmussar/Calendar_CodeAcademy","owner":"kmussar","html_url":"https://github.com/kmussar/Calendar_CodeAcademy","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"This is a a basic calendar program that the user will be able to interact with from the command line. The user can choose to (1) view the calendar, (2) add an event to the calendar, (3), update an existing event, or (4) delete an existing event. ","topics":[],"score":214921,"global_rank":252391,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":38350}]